The '''Chapel of St. Cornelius the Centurion''' is an [[Episcopal Church (United States)|Episcopal]] chapel on [[Governors Island]] in [[New York City]], United States. It is part of the [[parish]] of [[Trinity Church (Manhattan)|Trinity Church]] in [[Manhattan]] and is dedicated in honor of [[Cornelius the Centurion]]. The first building to bear this name was erected in 1847 thanks to the efforts of John McVickar, who had been serving as a chaplain at [[Fort Jay|Fort Columbus]] on Governors Island since 1844.{{refn|group=note|name=fort_name|The fort was renamed from Fort Columbus to Fort Jay in 1904.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=77}}}} By the beginning of the 20th century, this building was condemned and replaced by the current building, which was designed by architect [[Charles C. Haight]] and dedicated in 1906. The building was constructed in the [[English Gothic architecture|English Gothic]] and [[Gothic Revival architecture|Gothic Revival]] architectural styles.

For various periods in its history, chaplains have been provided from different sources, including from Trinity Church, the Army's chaplain services, and the [[United States Navy Chaplain Corps]]. Since the closure of the military outpost on Governors Island in 1996, the building has been under the sole ownership of Trinity Church. In the 21st century, the building has served as a venue for performing arts and art installations. As of 2014, it did not hold religious services.

== History ==
=== Early history ===
In 1844,{{sfn|Chorley|1947|p=69}}{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=155}} [[The Reverend]] John McVickar, a professor at [[Columbia College, Columbia University|Columbia College]] and [[Episcopal Church (United States)|Episcopalian]] priest, accepted an offer to serve as a [[chaplain]] at [[Fort Jay|Fort Columbus]] on [[Governors Island]].{{refn|group=note|name=fort_name}}{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=155}} McVickar initially held religious services in an office building, but in 1847, with support from [[Major general (United States)|Major General]] [[Winfield Scott]], he formulated plans for a dedicated [[chapel]] building at the fort.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=155}} He did not receive government funding for the building, but was able to raise money via donations from churchgoers in Manhattan.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=155}} McVickar also donated a significant amount of his own money towards the building's erection.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=155}} The building, designed by McVickar,{{sfn|Chorley|1947|p=69}} was dedicated as the Chapel of St. Cornelius the Centurion by Bishop [[William H. DeLancey]] of the [[Episcopal Diocese of Western New York]] on April 19, 1847.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} The construction cost several thousand dollars.{{refn|group=note|Sources vary on the cost of construction. In a 1950 book on the history of Trinity Church's parish, John A. Dix, the [[Churchwarden|senior warden]] of Trinity Church, stated that the construction cost approximately $2,500 (${{Inflation|index=US|value=2500|start_year=1847|r=-2|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}).{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}} However, in a 1973 book, journalist [[Clifford Phelps Morehouse]] gave the cost as roughly $4,500 (${{Inflation|index=US|value=4500|start_year=1847|r=-2|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}).{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} Both of these values are significantly lower than the cost of $25,000 (${{Inflation|index=US|value=25000|start_year=1847|r=-3|fmt=c}} in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}) that McVickar gave in a letter written to his son during the building's construction.{{sfn|Chorley|1947|pp=69–70}}}}

In 1850, McVickar's services expanded to include [[Liberty Island|Bedloe's Island]], where the Army had relocated some of the recruiting services.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}} In 1862, following the outbreak of the [[American Civil War]], the [[United States Army]] reworked its policy regarding chaplains, requiring civilian chaplains to reside permanently on their bases.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} Since McVickar was unable to reside permanently on the island, he resigned, with the Army stationing one of their own chaplains at the fort.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} He was initially succeeded by a man named Scudder,{{sfn|Smith|1923|p=185}} a minister of the [[Dutch Reformed Church]], who retired in 1865.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}} Scudder was replaced by James Armout Moore La Tourette of the Episcopal Church, who served until 1868.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}}{{sfn|Smith|1923|p=185}}

=== Parish of Trinity Church ===
[[File:Chapel of St. Cornelius the Centurion, c. 1900.jpg|thumb|The original chapel building, {{Circa|1900}}]]
The Army provided a military chaplain for the chapel for the duration of the war, but in 1868, they discontinued this program, stating that ministerial services should be provided by a local church elsewhere in [[New York City]].{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} That year, the [[clergy]] of [[Trinity Church (Manhattan)|Trinity Church]], geographically one of the closest churches to the chapel, entered into an agreement with the [[United States Department of War]] wherein the church would maintain a chaplain on the island if the government provided housing and other necessities that they would normally provide to military chaplains.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} Additionally, Trinity Church would receive free use of the chapel building for their religious services.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} In a 1973 book on the [[parish]]'s history, Episcopalian journalist [[Clifford Phelps Morehouse]] stated that the arrangement, which was formally agreed upon in August,{{sfn|Smith|1923|p=186}}{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=80}} was "unique among army posts".{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} St. Cornelius was one of a number of chapels added to the parish in the late 1860s, alongside [[St. Augustine's Church (Manhattan)|St. Augustine's]] and St. Chrysostom's.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=154–155}}

=== Current chapel building ===
In the early 20th century, the chapel building was [[Condemned property|condemned]], prompting the creation of a replacement building.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} Architect [[Charles C. Haight]] designed this new building.{{sfn|Dolkart|Postal|2004|p=366}} The [[cornerstone]] was laid on October 27, 1905, by [[Coadjutor Bishop]] [[David H. Greer]] of the [[Episcopal Diocese of New York]].{{sfn|Chorley|1947|p=72}} Coincidentally, Haight's father, [[Benjamin I. Haight]], had previously served as an assistant minister at Trinity Church.{{sfn|Chorley|1947|pp=71–72}} Work was completed in 1906.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} On October 19 of that year,{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=257}} the building was consecrated by Bishop Greer in a ceremony attended by both clergy of Trinity Church and military officials.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=210}} Greer substituted for Bishop [[Henry C. Potter]], who was unable to preside over the consecration due to a recent death in his family.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=258}} The sermon for the event was written by [[Morgan Dix]], Trinity Church's [[Rector (ecclesiastical)|rector]], and read by assistant rector [[William T. Manning]].{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=210}}

In November 1910, a military memorial service honoring American soldiers was held at the chapel, with several notable New Yorkers and members of the military in attendance.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1910}} Current military officers included Major General [[Frederick Dent Grant]] of the [[Department of the East]], and [[Lieutenant colonel (United States)|Lieutenant Colonels]] [[Adelbert Cronkhite]], [[John A. Hull]], and [[Isaac Littell]], while retired military members included [[Asa Bird Gardiner]], [[John White Moore]], [[Charles Augustus Schermerhorn]], and [[Andrew C. Zabriskie]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1910}}

==== Military flags and trophies ====
Following the dedication, the chapel's [[vicar]], Edmund Banks Smith, began a project to beautify the building by adding [[Military colours, standards and guidons|military flags]] and other decorations.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=210}} In 1907, the church accepted five flags from the [[1st Regiment of New York Volunteers]] that had been carried during the [[Mexican–American War]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1907}} They were presented to the chapel by five veterans of that regiment in a large ceremony attended by representatives of various military and civic organizations, including the [[Society of the Cincinnati]], the [[General Society of the War of 1812]], the [[Aztec Club of 1847]], and the [[Military Order of the Loyal Legion of the United States]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1907}} Notable individuals present, either in a military capacity or as representatives of the organizations, included Major General Grant, [[Admiral (United States)|Admiral]] [[Caspar F. Goodrich]], [[F. Burrall Hoffman]], [[Francis Key Pendleton]], [[Herbert L. Satterlee]], and [[Cornelius Vanderbilt III]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1907}}

In 1915, ''[[The New York Times]]'' published an article discussing the flags kept at the chapel, which by that time numbered over fifty.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1915}} Most of the larger flags were hung from the rafters in the [[nave]], while flags from the Mexican–American War were hung in the south [[transept]] and the [[cavalry]] [[Guidon (United States)|guidons]] were displayed in the [[Choir (architecture)|choir]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1915}} At the time, the oldest flag dated back to approximately 1787, while the most recent was a large American flag from the [[Boxer Rebellion]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1915}} In 1921, the [[British Army]] donated a regimental color from the [[King's Royal Rifle Corps]] to the chapel as a gesture of goodwill between the United Kingdom and the United States.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1921}} By 2010, the chapel housed 87 battle flags.{{sfn|White|Willensky|Leadon|2010|p=951}}

=== Army chaplains ===
In 1924, Smith retired.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=249}} Subsequently, the Army and Trinity Church came to a new agreement regarding the chapel's vicars.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=249–250}}{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} The Army would provide a military chaplain for the chapel who would be a priest in the Episcopal Church and would subsequently be made an honorary vicar of Trinity Church.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=156}} This arrangement existed for the duration of the Army's presence on the island.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|p=250}} In 1955, the first man was commissioned into the [[United States Army Nurse Corps]] in a ceremony at the chapel.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1955}}

=== Navy chaplains ===
On December 31, 1965, the Army closed its fort on Governors Island; the [[United States Coast Guard]] planned to use the facility starting the following June.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=293–294}} Around that time, a historical plaque was erected outside the chapel.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=293–294}} In the interim, the current Army vicar remained at the post until the Coast Guard took over.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=293–294}} Chaplains were subsequently supplied by the [[United States Navy Chaplain Corps]],{{sfn|TrinityChurchNYC.org|2014}} with the same agreement in place with Trinity that resulted in these chaplains being named honorary vicars.{{sfn|Morehouse|1973|pp=312–313}}

Starting in the 1970s, Trinity Church began to face financial difficulties that affected its administration of the chapel.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} In 1976, Trinity Church paid for a new lighting system for the chapel, but this would be the last major investment in the building for a decade.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} Following this, the building went into a state of disrepair, needing roof repairs and improvements to its pipe organ system.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} According to ''The New York Times'', Trinity Church found it difficult to finance repairs because the building was located on government property, while the government was unable to help with repairs due to regulations regarding privately owned structures.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} In 1980, the church began to lease the chapel to the government for $1 (equivalent to ${{Inflation|index=US|value=1|start_year=1980|r=2}} in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}) per year.{{sfn|TrinityChurchNYC.org|2014}}

In March 1986, Trinity Church agreed to turn over ownership of the building to the government, with a provision that the church would regain ownership if the building were not used for religious purposes.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} According to The Reverend Norman L. Cram Jr., the chaplain, "It's a necessary move, because the government has to own the chapel in order to put money into it. And the building really ought to be maintained by the government since it is used exclusively by government employees."{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} At the time, the building was valued at approximately $3 million (${{Inflation|index=US|value=3|start_year=1986|r=1}} million in {{Inflation/year|index=US}}).{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} The move was also part of a larger series of divestitures by Trinity Church going back to the 1970s, when the church began to suffer financially.{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} By the end of the year, the only chapel under Trinity's purview would be [[St. Paul's Chapel]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}}

=== Fort closure and recent history ===
[[File:St Cornelius Chapel, Governors Island 1.jpg|thumb|The chapel ''(interior pictured 2012)'' has hosted numerous public events in the 21st century, including several art installations.{{sfn|Vogel|2009}}{{sfn|Sierzputowski|2018}}]]
In 1996, the [[New York City Landmarks Preservation Commission]] declared the part of the island that included the chapel as the Governors Island [[Historic District]].{{sfn|Dolkart|Postal|2004|p=366}} That same year, the Coast Guard ceased use of the island and, as a result, the chapel came back under the ownership of Trinity Church.{{sfn|TrinityChurchNYC.org|2014}}

In 2005, the island opened to the public as a [[park]].{{sfn|Walsh|2011}} Since then, the chapel has been used for a number of public events. In 2008, the chapel held screenings as part of that year's [[African Film Festival, Inc.|African Film Festival]].{{sfn|Graeber|2008}} A year later, the [[Nonprofit organization|nonprofit group]] [[Creative Time]] held an expansive free [[art exhibition]] on the island.{{sfn|Vogel|2009}} [[Anthony McCall]] installed a [[light art]] piece inside the chapel and Klaus Weber displayed a [[wind chime]] outside the building.{{sfn|Smith|2009}}{{sfn|Kilgannon|2009}} In 2014, the [[Reggie Wilson/Fist and Heel Performance Group]] held a stage production at the chapel.{{sfn|Kourlas|2014}} By that year, the chapel was only one of two in the parish of Trinity Church, though it was not in active use.{{sfn|TrinityChurchNYC.org|2014}} [[Gia Kourlas]] of ''[[The New York Times]]'' described the building at that time as having "crumbling white walls".{{sfn|Kourlas|2014}} In 2018, the chapel was host to "The Eclipse",{{sfn|McDermon|2018}} an art installation by [[Jacob Hashimoto]] that consisted of thousands of paper kites displayed throughout the building.{{sfn|Boyer-Dry|Rao|2018}}{{sfn|Sierzputowski|2018}}

== Design ==
[[File:St Cornelius Governors Island jeh.JPG|thumb|The chapel in 2008]]
The chapel's architectural style has been described as both [[English Gothic architecture|English Gothic]]{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} and [[Gothic Revival architecture|Gothic Revival]].{{sfn|Dolkart|Postal|2004|p=366}}{{sfn|White|Willensky|Leadon|2010|p=951}} The exterior is clad in [[Course (architecture)|coursed]] and [[Rustication (architecture)|rusticated]] limestone.{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|pp=4, 15}} The overall shape of the building is generally [[Cruciform#Cruciform architectural plan|cruciform]], and it has length and width measurements of approximately {{Convert|106|ft|m}} by {{Convert|70|ft|m|abbr=on}}.{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|p=73}} The northwest corner has a three-story square tower containing the main entrance at ground level, while a four-[[Turret (architecture)|turret]] [[parapet]] caps it.{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|p=73}} The roof is made of [[slate]] and features [[copper]] finishings, including [[rain gutter]]s.{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|p=73}} There are 35 [[stained glass]] windows, many of which are [[Lancet window|lancet]]-shaped.{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|p=73}} On the east side of the building is the cornerstone, which bears the inscription "A.D. 1905".{{sfn|Hansen|Pearson|1996|p=73}}

The interior of the building consists of white walls and houses a [[pipe organ]].{{sfn|''The New York Times''|1986}} Additionally, there are three [[sedilia]] located in the [[Sanctuary#Sanctuary as area around the altar|sanctuary]].{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}} Of these, the main one is dedicated to McVickar,{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=79}} and the other two are dedicated to fellow vicars Davidson and La Tourette.{{sfn|Dix|1950|p=82}} McVickar's sedilia bears the following inscription:{{sfn|Smith|1923|p=185}}{{sfn|Chorley|1947|p=70}}

{{Blockquote|text=In / Memory of / John McVickar / Priest and Doctor / Born 1787, Died 1868 / Chaplain of this Post / 1844–1862 / By whose wisdom and / liberality the first / Chapel of St. Cornelius / the Centurion was / erected in 1846 / The law of truth / was in his mouth, / and iniquity / was not found in / his lips.}}

La Tourette's sedilia says the following:{{sfn|Smith|1923|p=186}}

{{Blockquote|text=In / Memory of / James Armour Moore / La Tourette / Priest. / Born 1826. Died 1891. / Chaplain of this / Post, / 1865–1868. / Faithful in the dis- / charge of duty, notably / in the siege of Asiatic / cholera of 1866. / Instant in season / Out of season.}}

Finally, Davidson's bears the following inscription:{{sfn|Chorley|1947|p=71}}

{{Blockquote|text=In Memory of the Rev. / Alexander Davidson / Chaplain at Fort Columbus / New York Harbor / Who died of Yellow Fever / Sept. A. D. 1870. / Though absent on sick leave when the disease broke out he came back, and while ministering to the sick and dying, was himself struck down and thus gave his life for his Brethren.}}

The chapel is located near the intersection of Barry Road and Evans Road,{{sfn|White|Willensky|Leadon|2010|p=951}} at the west end of Nolan Park.{{sfn|Walsh|2010}}{{sfn|Walsh|2011}}

